      "title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"daff6aa5f9c7fe84\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nChromosomal abnormalities associated with a single umbilical artery - PubMed\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"daff6aa5f9c7fe84\">>>",
      "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1494550/",
      "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2b439458b7e3b2c0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe abnormal karyotypes found to be associated with a single umbilical artery were <strong>trisomy 18 (n = 5), monosomy X (n = 2), triploidy (n = 1), sex chromosome (47,XYY; n = 1) and translocation (46t(X,5)(q13p15);n = 1)</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2b439458b7e3b2c0\">>>",
      "siteName": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"
    },
    {
      "title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"596a63867eb4f566\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSingle umbilical artery - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"596a63867eb4f566\">>>",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_umbilical_artery",
      "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"826d0472991463ac\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nOccasionally, during pregnancy, there is a single umbilical artery (SUA) present in the umbilical cord, as opposed to the usual two. This is sometimes also called a two-vessel umbilical cord, or two-vessel cord. Approximately, this affects between 1 in 100 and 1 in 500 pregnancies, making it the most common umbilical abnormality.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"826d0472991463ac\">>>",
      "published": "October 3, 2025",
      "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"
    },
    {
      "title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f576b63fc8ca85b0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSingle umbilical artery and risk of congenital malformation: population‐based study in Norway - Ebbing - 2020 - Ultrasound in Obstetrics & Gynecology - Wiley Online Library\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f576b63fc8ca85b0\">>>",
      "url": "https://obgyn.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/uog.20359",
      "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0446be4d9084b4fd\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSUA is associated strongly with <strong>gastrointestinal atresia or stenosis</strong>, suggesting common developmental mechanisms. The increased risk of recurrence of SUA suggests that genetic and/or persisting environmental factors influence the risk.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0446be4d9084b4fd\">>>",
      "published": "March 31, 2020",
      "siteName": "obgyn.onlinelibrary.wiley.com"
    }
  ]
